            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to take across, then the Latin for to swim to safety."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: portus, portūs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "portus, portūs — \"a harbour, a port\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tandem nāvis in portum intrat, et nautae gaudent. At last the ship comes into the harbour, and the sailors are glad."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fourth declension, masculine: portus, portūs m. Figurative too: a safe place after trouble. English port comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "portus is inherited from Proto-Italic portus, from PIE pértus 'crossing, passage' (EtymDB), the same root as porta 'gate'. English port comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
